 "conclusion": "Counting readout reuses the PBNN popcount periphery and reads the ensemble losslessly, but pays per-device sensing energy: at the canonical E = 96 a single-strobe popcount costs 24x the shot-noise-matched SAR conversion (b* = 5) for lower MC (counting-1 1.99 vs SAR 2.33; the single strobe also FORFEITS the window-integration gain of +0.34 MC that the analog integrator collects passively, N_eff <= 1.57 at dt = 25 ns). Every effective bit costs 4x devices AND 4x sensing energy (b_eff = 0.5 log2 E), while the SAR price is b-linear. Verdict: the seemingly obvious 'reuse the PBNN counters for RC' route is energetically excluded whenever a column-shared SAR is available; it survives only as the area-minimal single-periphery option. Side finding: the mean-field MC at this same operating point (4.92) is an infinite-ensemble ceiling; at physical E the ensemble noise -- acting through readout AND recurrence -- binds MC, not ADC resolution."
